1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,184
The school next asks you to recognize gastrointestinal bleeding and
bowel obstruction. One can hide blood loss; the other can hide fluid
loss inside a distended bowel.

2
00:00:11,184 --> 00:00:23,064
Both can present with vomiting, pain, poor perfusion or reduced urine.
The shared consequences do not make their cause, definitive treatment
or medication choices identical.

3
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:34,128
We will follow mechanisms, recognize red flags and build a safe
assessment and conveyance plan. An exact source diagnosis is often
unavailable in the field.

4
00:00:34,128 --> 00:00:46,944
Use the original unfolding cases and the assigned CPSC reading to
connect the patient, visible findings and any existing devices. These
are preparation, not school assessment answers.

5
00:00:46,944 --> 00:00:58,320
The school uses the classic ligament-of-Treitz boundary near the
duodenojejunal junction. Upper sources include the esophagus, stomach
and duodenum proximal to that boundary.

6
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:09,264
In that classic framework, distal bowel is grouped as lower.
Contemporary resources often discuss small-bowel bleeding separately
from colonic and rectal lower bleeding.

7
00:01:09,264 --> 00:01:20,496
Know which framework the school question is using, but do not let the
label overstate a field diagnosis. The receiving team benefits from
the actual vomit and stool description.

8
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:28,848
A boundary helps organize likely causes. It does not make the blood
color a reliable map or determine how sick the person is.

9
00:01:28,848 --> 00:01:41,424
Hematemesis means vomiting blood. Fresh red material and coffee-ground
material can both be important; coffee grounds reflect altered blood,
often after contact with gastric contents.

10
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:52,608
Melena is black, tarry stool associated with digested blood. It
commonly suggests an upper source, but does not prove one exact site
or that bleeding has stopped.

11
00:01:52,608 --> 00:02:02,520
Hematochezia describes red or maroon rectal blood. Although often
lower, a brisk upper bleed can move rapidly and appear this way.

12
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:14,520
Ask whether the finding was observed or reported, and note possible
nonblood color changes from foods or medicines. Describe uncertainty
rather than declaring every dark stool melena.

13
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:26,928
Peptic ulcers can erode a vessel, with risks including Helicobacter
pylori and NSAID exposure. A prior pattern of meal-related pain is not
required before a serious bleed.

14
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:38,736
Portal hypertension can produce enlarged esophageal or gastric veins.
Rupture can cause major hemorrhage, and impaired liver function can
complicate coagulation and recovery.

15
00:02:38,736 --> 00:02:52,200
A Mallory-Weiss mucosal tear can follow forceful retching. It is
different from a full-thickness esophageal rupture, which may present
with severe chest or upper-abdominal pain and systemic deterioration.

16
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:04,272
These histories refine suspicion, but absence of liver disease,
alcohol use or known ulcers does not clear the patient. Treat the
current airway and perfusion consequences.

17
00:03:04,272 --> 00:03:15,792
Diverticular bleeding can be painless yet large. Pain severity and
blood-loss severity are separate questions, so do not use a
comfortable abdomen as a stability test.

18
00:03:15,792 --> 00:03:28,752
Vascular lesions can bleed intermittently; inflammatory, ischemic or
infectious colitis and malignancy have different patterns and risks.
The field may not distinguish them definitively.

19
00:03:28,752 --> 00:03:40,440
Hemorrhoids and fissures can produce bright red blood. A history of
either does not explain away new heavy bleeding, fainting, poor
perfusion or a major change from baseline.

20
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:51,000
Use the history to identify possibilities and risk. Avoid reassuring
the patient that it is only hemorrhoids without appropriate assessment
and follow-up.

21
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:01,008
Overt bleeding is visible. Occult bleeding is not visible to the eye
and is commonly detected during evaluation of anemia or a positive
stool test.

22
00:04:01,008 --> 00:04:13,032
Slow chronic loss can lead to fatigue, pallor, breathlessness and
reduced exercise tolerance. A change may seem gradual until another
illness reduces the remaining reserve.

23
00:04:13,032 --> 00:04:24,144
A major acute bleed can also be unrecognized because the blood has not
yet been seen or reported. Do not confuse that situation with proof of
a small chronic occult loss.

24
00:04:24,144 --> 00:04:36,240
Shock findings with a compatible story require action without waiting
for stool testing or visible blood. A field fecal test is not a
prerequisite for stabilization or conveyance.

25
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:47,592
Blood loss can reduce venous return, ventricular filling and stroke
volume. Sympathetic compensation may preserve cuff pressure through
heart-rate and vascular changes.

26
00:04:47,592 --> 00:05:00,936
Look at the whole pattern: pallor, skin temperature, pulse quality,
dizziness, weakness, respiratory change and mentation. A normal
pressure does not prove adequate organ perfusion.

27
00:05:00,936 --> 00:05:11,256
Medicines, age, autonomic disease and baseline physiology can modify
the pulse response. Absence of tachycardia cannot rule out hemorrhage.

28
00:05:11,256 --> 00:05:23,208
Do not force the patient to stand to demonstrate orthostatic change or
assign an exact blood-loss percentage from one observation. Use a safe
supported move and repeat meaningful measurements.

29
00:05:23,208 --> 00:05:33,648
Pulse oximetry estimates saturation of available hemoglobin. It does
not measure how much hemoglobin is present or how much blood is
reaching tissues.

30
00:05:33,648 --> 00:05:45,480
A patient with anemia or low circulating volume may have a normal
saturation and poor oxygen delivery. Interpret the number with
perfusion, function and the bleeding story.

31
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:58,608
In acute blood loss, an early hemoglobin concentration may initially
appear acceptable before redistribution and fluid effects evolve. A
single result cannot cancel ongoing hemorrhage concern.

32
00:05:58,608 --> 00:06:10,872
Give oxygen when indicated through the actual pathway and support
ventilation if inadequate. Oxygen and crystalloid do not restore lost
red-cell carrying capacity or stop the source.

33
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:21,696
Ask when the change began, how often blood or dark material appeared
and whether it continues. Separate a witnessed event from a patient or
companion report.

34
00:06:21,696 --> 00:06:33,456
Ask about fainting, exertional breathlessness, chest discomfort,
weakness and new confusion. These can reveal consequences even when
the visible amount seems small.

35
00:06:33,456 --> 00:06:45,000
Review prior ulcer or bleeding, liver disease, renal or cardiac
illness and recent vomiting. Do not require a classic past history
before taking a new bleed seriously.

36
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:59,472
Identify anticoagulants, antiplatelets and NSAIDs with the last known
dose. Do not independently reverse or permanently discontinue a
prescription; provide accurate information for urgent clinical review.

37
00:06:59,472 --> 00:07:09,576
A sixty-four-year-old reports several black tarry stools and
increasing weakness. The history includes apixaban and recent
nonprescription ibuprofen.

38
00:07:09,576 --> 00:07:20,184
The patient is pale, with pulse one hundred ten, breathing twenty-
four, pressure one hundred sixteen over seventy-two and saturation
ninety-eight on room air.

39
00:07:20,184 --> 00:07:30,408
The patient feels faint when trying to move. The preserved pressure
and saturation do not cancel concern about gastrointestinal bleeding
and impaired delivery.

40
00:07:30,408 --> 00:07:42,288
State the immediate priorities before naming the presumed source:
supported safe positioning, primary assessment, serial perfusion
checks, indicated care and timely conveyance.

41
00:07:42,288 --> 00:07:53,784
Do not stand this patient to prove an orthostatic result. Explain the
concern and plan a supported movement with the team, appropriate
monitoring and airway equipment available.

42
00:07:53,784 --> 00:08:05,520
Repeat airway, breathing and circulation assessment, with relevant
glucose and abdominal examination. Track pulse quality, mental status
and pressure with times.

43
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:16,512
Obtain indicated access and provide permitted support with
reassessment. Do not delay conveyance for a perfect venous setup or to
finish an arbitrary fluid volume.

44
00:08:16,512 --> 00:08:29,424
Pre-alert the receiving team with suspected bleeding, anticoagulant
and NSAID history, faintness and actual observations. Leave the exact
source uncertain unless there is evidence to establish it.

45
00:08:29,424 --> 00:08:40,248
Prepare suction, an emesis receptacle, suitable PPE and the airway
equipment you can use. Active blood or vomit can rapidly alter the
initial assessment.

46
00:08:40,248 --> 00:08:51,480
Use a supported, tolerated position and enable drainage if vomiting,
with injury needs respected. A mask must not prevent immediate access
to clear the airway.

47
00:08:51,480 --> 00:09:02,376
Check depth and effort as well as saturation. If ventilation is
inadequate, provide effective support and early advanced help rather
than waiting for a low oxygen number.

48
00:09:02,376 --> 00:09:14,544
No airway device guarantees protection against aspiration. Reassess
clearance and ventilation after any change, especially with declining
responsiveness or continuing hematemesis.

49
00:09:14,544 --> 00:09:24,696
Explain the examination, obtain the appropriate consent and protect
dignity. Expose only what is needed and use a position the patient can
tolerate.

50
00:09:24,696 --> 00:09:36,912
Observe distention, scars, symmetry and how movement affects pain.
Gentle assessment can identify tenderness or guarding without
repeatedly reproducing severe pain.

51
00:09:36,912 --> 00:09:50,592
The old deep five-to-ten-centimeter rebound instruction is not a
routine field maneuver. Do not repeatedly press a suspected pulsatile
mass or require a digital rectal examination to justify urgent care.

52
00:09:50,592 --> 00:10:02,208
Bowel sounds may add information but cannot clear obstruction or
ischemia. Document what was actually assessed and let current
instability take priority over a lengthy examination.

53
00:10:02,208 --> 00:10:12,936
Use serial observations with the frequency required by the patient and
local standards. A changing pattern is more useful than one
comfortable number copied forward.

54
00:10:12,936 --> 00:10:23,640
Link each observation to the time, position and intervention. If the
patient becomes confused or breathing worsens, repeat the primary
assessment immediately.

55
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:35,352
Blood mixed with water, food or stool is difficult to quantify.
Describe what you saw and give an honest estimate or uncertainty
rather than a precise invented volume.

56
00:10:35,352 --> 00:10:45,096
A small visible amount does not measure all hidden or previous loss.
Likewise, a red toilet is not proof of a particular number of liters.

57
00:10:45,096 --> 00:10:55,272
GI hemorrhage cannot usually be controlled by external pressure.
Arrange timely definitive evaluation while supporting the current
circulation and breathing.

58
00:10:55,272 --> 00:11:05,568
Use indicated vascular access and the actual local fluid plan,
comparing mentation, pulses, pressure and respiratory function after
each step.

59
00:11:05,568 --> 00:11:19,392
Crystalloid can support volume temporarily, but excess can dilute and
worsen congestion. It does not replace red cells or close a bleeding
vessel, so an empty bag is not the treatment endpoint.

60
00:11:19,392 --> 00:11:30,816
The hospital may need blood support, endoscopic treatment or another
source-control process. Communicate risk early rather than allowing
minor setup tasks to consume that time.

61
00:11:30,816 --> 00:11:43,344
Appropriate analgesia need not be withheld because it supposedly masks
all abdominal disease. Symptom relief supports humane care while
examination and reassessment continue.

62
00:11:43,344 --> 00:11:58,464
Choose only an authorized option suitable for this patient. Active
bleeding, kidney disease, allergy, interactions, impaired breathing
and pressure can all matter; hypotension is not the only possible
contraindication.

63
00:11:58,464 --> 00:12:09,264
Gross abdominal distention is a current nitrous-oxide
contraindication. Do not use a familiar pain medicine automatically
just because it worked on a previous patient.

64
00:12:09,264 --> 00:12:21,864
Use the indicated antiemetic and reassess, but prepare for vomiting
despite treatment. Improvement of pain or nausea does not prove the
dangerous underlying problem has resolved.

65
00:12:21,864 --> 00:12:32,832
The currently checked BCEHS tranexamic-acid monograph explicitly lists
nontraumatic gastrointestinal hemorrhage, such as a bleeding ulcer or
varices, as a contraindication.

66
00:12:32,832 --> 00:12:42,384
Do not borrow the trauma or maternal indication merely because this
patient is bleeding. Cause-specific eligibility remains necessary.

67
00:12:42,384 --> 00:12:56,352
The original HALT-IT trial did not demonstrate a reduction in bleeding
death with its studied regimen and found more venous thromboembolic
events. This supports avoiding a uniform all-bleeding treatment
assumption.

68
00:12:56,352 --> 00:13:06,384
Continue the indicated basic support, consultation and urgent
conveyance plan. An old textbook statement about occasional use is not
current permission.

69
00:13:06,384 --> 00:13:16,512
A mechanical obstruction physically limits passage. Causes can be
within the lumen, in the bowel wall or outside it, including adhesions
from prior surgery.

70
00:13:16,512 --> 00:13:25,200
A hernia can trap bowel, and a twisted segment or closed loop can
threaten the blood supply as well as the passage of contents.

71
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:37,920
Fluid and gas collect upstream, increasing distention and vomiting
risk. The location and completeness influence the pattern, but field
symptoms cannot define the anatomy precisely.

72
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:46,584
Ask about prior surgery, hernia and previous obstruction. A patient
without those histories can still have a serious obstruction.

73
00:13:46,584 --> 00:13:58,848
Functional ileus means impaired bowel propulsion without a single
physical blockage. Surgery, systemic illness, medicines and
electrolyte problems can contribute.

74
00:13:58,848 --> 00:14:11,736
Distention, discomfort, nausea, vomiting and reduced bowel output can
resemble mechanical obstruction. Quiet bowel sounds do not establish
which mechanism is present.

75
00:14:11,736 --> 00:14:23,232
Both need evaluation in the right clinical context, with immediate
support for airway, perfusion and other deterioration. Do not assume
the functional label makes the patient safe.

76
00:14:23,232 --> 00:14:33,192
The school small-versus-large pattern comparison is useful
preparation. Keep it separate from claiming that examination alone has
excluded a surgical cause.

77
00:14:33,192 --> 00:14:44,808
Small-bowel obstruction often relates to adhesions or hernia. Crampy
pain reflects contractions against resistance, and vomiting may be an
early prominent symptom.

78
00:14:44,808 --> 00:14:56,088
The more proximal the obstruction, the less dramatic the abdominal
distention may initially be. A flat-looking abdomen cannot exclude a
dangerous upper small-bowel problem.

79
00:14:56,088 --> 00:15:08,064
Losses through vomiting and sequestration can reduce effective volume.
Ask about intake, emesis, urine and the time course rather than only
counting visible episodes.

80
00:15:08,064 --> 00:15:19,056
A change from intermittent cramps to severe continuous pain is
concerning, especially with tenderness or poor perfusion. It is not
necessary to wait for feculent vomiting.

81
00:15:19,056 --> 00:15:30,432
Large-bowel obstruction can relate to a colorectal tumor, a volvulus
or a stricture. There may be a gradual bowel-habit change, although
some causes present suddenly.

82
00:15:30,432 --> 00:15:40,464
Distention is often marked, with reduced stool or flatus. Vomiting may
be less prominent early than in a proximal small-bowel obstruction.

83
00:15:40,464 --> 00:15:52,224
These are tendencies, not a checklist that safely identifies the
segment. Severe distention and deterioration can require urgent
evaluation even with little vomiting.

84
00:15:52,224 --> 00:16:03,072
Do not label large-bowel obstruction slow and therefore safe.
Ischemia, perforation, aspiration and shock can occur in either broad
category.

85
00:16:03,072 --> 00:16:15,480
Partial obstruction may permit some passage, and stool already beyond
a new blockage can still be passed. A recent bowel movement or a small
episode of diarrhea does not exclude obstruction.

86
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:27,264
Similarly, hearing bowel sounds does not prove normal passage, while
not hearing them briefly does not establish paralysis. The noisy
environment further limits confidence.

87
00:16:27,264 --> 00:16:37,584
Ask about the last normal stool and flatus, not only whether anything
passed. Compare that with distention, pain, vomiting, surgery and the
clinical trend.

88
00:16:37,584 --> 00:16:47,064
Do not delay necessary transport for prolonged auscultation or assume
symptom improvement after an antiemetic has removed a mechanical
problem.

89
00:16:47,064 --> 00:16:57,552
The gut normally handles large amounts of fluid through secretion and
absorption. Obstruction changes movement and absorption, with fluid
accumulating upstream.

90
00:16:57,552 --> 00:17:08,520
Fluid can become sequestered in the lumen and edematous bowel wall.
The school calls this third spacing: fluid is unavailable to maintain
effective circulation.

91
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:18,552
Vomiting adds external water and electrolyte loss. A distended abdomen
therefore does not prove the person has sufficient circulating volume.

92
00:17:18,552 --> 00:17:31,368
Assess perfusion and breathing, and provide only the indicated
response-directed support. The diagram explains risk; it does not
prescribe one fluid dose for every distended patient.

93
00:17:31,368 --> 00:17:41,568
Increasing pressure or a trapped loop can impede venous drainage.
Swelling can further impair perfusion, and arterial supply may become
inadequate.

94
00:17:41,568 --> 00:17:53,400
This can produce ischemia and eventually tissue necrosis. A
strangulated hernia or closed-loop process may threaten blood flow
before the typical full symptom pattern appears.

95
00:17:53,400 --> 00:18:07,776
Concerning change includes severe continuous pain, tenderness,
guarding, worsening perfusion or altered function. Normal temperature
and a soft abdomen do not guarantee that ischemia is absent.

96
00:18:07,776 --> 00:18:19,320
The field task is urgent recognition and escalation, not proving every
surgical mechanism. Do not force a painful hernia back or wait for a
dramatic late abdomen.

97
00:18:19,320 --> 00:18:32,256
A compromised bowel wall can perforate, allowing irritating contents
and bacteria into the peritoneal cavity. Peritonitis is inflammation
of that lining and can have several causes.

98
00:18:32,256 --> 00:18:42,960
Pain may become persistent and worsen with movement, with tenderness
or guarding. Do not deliberately reproduce repeated rebound pain to
prove the emergency.

99
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:55,512
Inflammation, leakage, infection and poor circulation can overlap. A
patient may be deteriorating before fever, rigid abdomen or critical
hypotension becomes obvious.

100
00:18:55,512 --> 00:19:07,320
Request appropriate help, prepare airway and perfusion support and
notify the receiving team early. Absence of one classic sign should
not postpone definitive assessment.

101
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:17,472
Establish the pain onset and whether cramps have changed to persistent
severe pain. Ask how vomiting, distention and output evolved together.

102
00:19:17,472 --> 00:19:29,424
Ask about the last normal stool and flatus, the character of vomit,
intake and urine. Avoid treating one bowel movement or one normal
temperature as reassurance.

103
00:19:29,424 --> 00:19:42,456
Ask about surgery, hernia, cancer, ostomy and prior episodes, with the
actual baseline. The patient may know their anatomy and device plan
better than a generic label describes.

104
00:19:42,456 --> 00:19:54,840
Review illness and medication changes, especially agents that affect
motility or fluid balance. Use this information to refine the problem,
not to declare its cause from a single clue.

105
00:19:54,840 --> 00:20:05,208
A fifty-three-year-old with prior abdominal surgery has crampy pain,
repeated vomiting and increasing distention. There was a small loose
stool eight hours ago.

106
00:20:05,208 --> 00:20:15,672
Now flatus and urine are reduced. Pulse is one hundred twelve,
pressure one hundred eight over sixty-eight and temperature thirty-
seven point one.

107
00:20:15,672 --> 00:20:26,160
The small stool does not exclude partial or newly developing
obstruction. The loss history and reduced urine raise concern about
effective volume and renal perfusion.

108
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:40,008
Assess current ABCs, perfusion, pain and relevant glucose/rhythm,
support safely and arrange timely evaluation. Do not give a laxative
or a trial meal to test the diagnosis.

109
00:20:40,008 --> 00:20:53,592
During preparation, pain becomes severe and constant, tenderness
increases and the patient looks paler. Pulse rises to one hundred
twenty-four and pressure falls to ninety-six over sixty.

110
00:20:53,592 --> 00:21:05,760
This worsening trend matters more than the earlier normal temperature
or small stool. Possible ischemia, perforation, volume loss and
another cause must remain open.

111
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:17,496
Repeat the primary assessment, provide indicated support and seek
advanced resources without delaying urgent conveyance. Update the
receiving team with the change and concern.

112
00:21:17,496 --> 00:21:28,608
A field label cannot establish whether surgery is needed. The safe
decision is urgent definitive assessment, with actual treatment and
response documented en route.

113
00:21:28,608 --> 00:21:40,848
Prepare for further vomiting with suction and safe positioning. Assess
airway protection and effective ventilation repeatedly, especially if
the patient becomes fatigued or confused.

114
00:21:40,848 --> 00:21:52,752
Use indicated access and fluid support according to the current local
plan. Reassess perfusion and breathing because congestion risk or
another diagnosis may alter the response.

115
00:21:52,752 --> 00:22:04,584
Provide authorized, appropriate symptom relief rather than withholding
all medication out of fear of masking. Improvement of nausea does not
remove the need for evaluation.

116
00:22:04,584 --> 00:22:18,792
Red flags such as persistent severe pain, peritoneal concern, shock or
altered function require early escalation and notification. Definitive
decompression or surgery belongs to the appropriate clinical setting.

117
00:22:18,792 --> 00:22:28,968
Withhold routine food and drink in suspected obstruction or active
vomiting while arranging clinical evaluation. Do not try to flush the
blockage with water.

118
00:22:28,968 --> 00:22:40,128
Do not improvise laxatives, enemas or forceful reduction of a painful
hernia. A suspected blockage needs proper evaluation, not a trial to
provoke output.

119
00:22:40,128 --> 00:22:51,264
Gross distention excludes nitrous oxide in current local guidance.
Hospital NG decompression is not a blanket independent PCP insertion
instruction.

120
00:22:51,264 --> 00:23:03,648
Nothing-by-mouth precautions do not mean ignoring a separate time-
critical threat. Use the appropriate safe route and clinical advice
for essential indicated treatment, including a glucose emergency.

121
00:23:03,648 --> 00:23:15,216
Ask about the actual device, purpose and normal output. An ileostomy,
colostomy, urinary diversion, catheter or feeding tube does not tell
you the whole anatomy from its name.

122
00:23:15,216 --> 00:23:25,176
Trace visible tubing and check for traction, a kink or a collection
problem within your competence. Reassess the patient even if one
external problem is found.

123
00:23:25,176 --> 00:23:36,768
Do not break a closed system, feed through an unverified tube or
remove a drain simply to simplify transport. Secure and support the
actual system according to its plan.

124
00:23:36,768 --> 00:23:50,136
A new cessation of output with pain, distention, bleeding or
deterioration needs urgent review. The paired CPSC guide connects
these observations with safe transfer and device boundaries.

125
00:23:50,136 --> 00:24:02,376
Abdominal symptoms can arise from aortic disease, impaired bowel blood
flow or another vascular emergency. A pulsatile mass or unequal arm
pressures are not mandatory findings.

126
00:24:02,376 --> 00:24:17,280
ACS can present with epigastric discomfort, nausea or weakness. Obtain
indicated ECG and cardiac assessment without assuming every abdominal
patient should automatically receive aspirin or nitrate.

127
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:29,136
Consider pregnancy-related bleeding, pelvic disease, urinary retention
and renal illness when relevant. Ask respectfully about the actual
anatomy, dates and symptoms.

128
00:24:29,136 --> 00:24:41,712
Check glucose when indicated and keep infection and other metabolic
causes open. A GI symptom label cannot explain away new focal
neurology or impaired consciousness.

129
00:24:41,712 --> 00:24:54,000
Older, immunosuppressed or neuropathic patients may have less obvious
pain, fever or abdominal signs. A subtle examination can coexist with
serious disease.

130
00:24:54,000 --> 00:25:03,696
Cardiac, kidney and liver disease can reduce reserve and complicate
fluids or medicines. Ask what has changed from the individual
baseline.

131
00:25:03,696 --> 00:25:15,792
Use accessible communication and appropriate interpretation, with a
chosen support person when agreed. Do not equate difficulty giving a
history with incapacity or dismiss the report.

132
00:25:15,792 --> 00:25:27,864
Support dignity during soiling or vomiting and document factual
findings. Treatment urgency should reflect clinical need rather than
age stereotypes or embarrassment about symptoms.

133
00:25:27,864 --> 00:25:39,984
A bleed can reduce effective volume and renal perfusion. Obstruction
can add vomiting and sequestration, producing a similar urine-history
clue through a different initial mechanism.

134
00:25:39,984 --> 00:25:49,752
If ischemia or perforation develops, inflammation and infection can
add. Do not assume that one shock label contains the entire
explanation.

135
00:25:49,752 --> 00:26:02,040
The priorities remain current airway, ventilation and perfusion, with
cause-sensitive permitted care and prompt definitive evaluation.
Response must be measured, not promised.

136
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:14,280
Ask What Else when findings or response do not fit. Examples include
aortic disease, cardiac ischemia, ectopic bleeding, glucose
disturbance or a toxic exposure.

137
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:23,568
Lead with the current concern, such as suspected GI hemorrhage with
near-syncope or possible obstruction with worsening constant pain and
perfusion.

138
00:26:23,568 --> 00:26:33,456
Include the symptom timeline, medicines, comorbidities and actual loss
description. Make clear which findings were observed and which were
reported.

139
00:26:33,456 --> 00:26:46,224
State serial values and function, the interventions with times and the
actual response. A pressure trend and change in mentation can be more
useful than an invented source certainty.

140
00:26:46,224 --> 00:26:57,168
Give the arrival estimate and requested preparation, with remaining
uncertainty. Update the alert if bleeding, breathing or perfusion
changes during conveyance.

141
00:26:57,168 --> 00:27:07,800
For the bleeding case, explain why melena supports concern but does
not prove a source, and why normal pressure and saturation do not
establish adequate delivery.

142
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:17,832
For the obstruction case, explain why a recent small stool does not
clear it, and how a larger bowel can be accompanied by reduced
effective circulating volume.

143
00:27:17,832 --> 00:27:28,152
State the safe examination, immediate support, medication checks and
transport priority. Include an action you will avoid and the specific
reason.

144
00:27:28,152 --> 00:27:40,224
Then explain how worsening constant pain, altered function, new airway
difficulty or a poor response changes urgency. Use the paired reading
to repair any missing mechanism.

145
00:27:40,224 --> 00:28:28,440
Correct the source drop-factor unit: tubing calibration is drops per
milliliter, not drops per minute. For gravity, drops/minute equals
milliliters/hour times drops/milliliter divided by sixty. Original
calculation: an authorized 87 mL/hour through a verified 15 drops/mL
set gives 21.75, approximately 22 drops/minute using the trained
rounding method. Count over a full minute and verify the patient and
delivered volume. A pump uses mL/hour; do not enter 22 as its rate or
infer the set calibration from its appearance. An arithmetic answer
does not establish that fluid is indicated.

146
00:28:28,440 --> 00:29:10,608
Gravity flow changes with bag height, patient position, bends, lumen
diameter, viscosity, clamp movement and infiltration. Reassess after
the arm or stretcher moves; an initially correct clamp setting is not
a guarantee. The dated source TKVO20-50 mL/hour, fixed cannula-change
intervals and 24-hour bag statements are source/agency examples, not
one universal field rate or a proof of sterility until a deadline.
Distinguish product expiry, time since access and contamination.
Clinical condition, actual device instructions and the prescribed plan
determine action.

147
00:29:10,608 --> 00:30:00,456
If a transfusion reaction is suspected, stop the suspected transfusion
and obtain the immediate clinical/transfusion-service response while
supporting ABCs. Preserve necessary vascular access using the
authorized compatible plan; do not flush the remaining suspect blood
into the patient. Recheck identity, document
product/times/symptoms/response and preserve the bag/set for the
investigation process. The source mild-fever continuation note is not
permission to continue or restart without assessment and an explicit
treating-team plan. Its IV-epinephrine equipment sentence is not an
ordinary PCP IV epinephrine order; use the actual local pathway for a
recognized anaphylactic threat.

148
00:30:00,456 --> 00:30:43,056
Original integration: a patient with abdominal illness, a central line
and a nutrition infusion develops a new fever and reduced
responsiveness during transfer. Do not assume the line alone proves
bloodstream infection or that stopping every infusion is harmless.
Support immediate physiology, check glucose, compare actual baseline
and inspect the visible system. Obtain a trained plan for any
suspected source or critical-infusion interruption and arrange urgent
evaluation. Handover includes every route/product/rate, last known
normal function, new findings, actions, response and remaining
uncertainty.

149
00:30:43,056 --> 00:30:53,304
You should now describe the school upper/lower bleeding framework,
major causes, overt and occult loss and important exceptions to color-
based assumptions.

150
00:30:53,304 --> 00:31:05,544
You should compare small and large bowel patterns, explain third
spacing and recognize possible ischemia, perforation and systemic
deterioration without demanding every classic sign.

151
00:31:05,544 --> 00:31:16,872
Your care plan should protect airway and perfusion, relieve symptoms
safely, avoid unnecessary provocation and use the actual local drug
and procedure boundaries.

152
00:31:16,872 --> 00:31:28,992
Finish with an accurate changing-patient handover and the bilingual
reading, then move to the school endocrine and integrated medical
requirements. Practical competence still needs supervised performance.
